# Limitations

What this project does not do, and where its numbers would mislead if read too literally. Each item says what was simplified, why, and which way it is likely to bias the results. Numbers are computed by `scripts/render_docs.py` from `outputs/` and `docs/data/`; headline numbers are the v2 holdout (seeds {{RESULT:seeds.holdout}}, central world) unless labelled "selection". Plan names: {{RESULT:v21.name.P0}}, {{RESULT:v21.name.P1}}, {{RESULT:v21.name.P2}} and {{RESULT:v21.name.I125}}.

Every headline number is {{RESULT:v21.scope}}. {{RESULT:v21.ranges}}

---

## Data

**1. The data is synthetic.** The airline, fleet, part numbers, removals, repair orders and AOG events are generated. Only station coordinates are REAL. Every parameter is ASSUMED, chosen to be order-of-magnitude plausible, and listed in the [assumption register](docs/assumption_register.md). Results show how the methods behave under those assumptions; they are not a measurement of any airline. A v1 world was archived after a domain review; see [archive/v1](archive/v1/README.md).

**2. Simulated rates are not reliability data.** MTBUR, NFF shares, condemnation rates and repair TATs are drawn from configured ranges. They must never be quoted as measured component reliability.

**3. The models are tested on a world the author designed.** The generator draws removals from exposure ÷ MTBUR, which is exactly the structure the reliability-exposure forecast assumes. That gives it a home advantage. NFF is generated, but maintenance-induced removals and fleet campaigns are not.

**4. The vendor-quote error is assumed, and was set by the calibration.** The quoted MTBUR is {{RESULT:config.quote_noise}}. These settings were the calibration lever (V2-SIM-CAL), so P0's performance, and the size of P2's improvement, depend on them. {{RESULT:v21.calibration}}

**5. Demand is unconstrained and uncensored.** Every requirement is recorded whether or not stock existed. Real consumable issue histories are often censored by stockouts.

**6. Two years of history.** Parts with very few removals cannot be forecast from their own history; they rely on the quoted MTBUR.

## Operations

**7. No exposure feedback from groundings.** A grounded aircraft keeps accruing its scheduled flying (protection by spare aircraft or sub-charter is assumed). In reality a grounded aircraft does not fly, so removals on that tail pause. This keeps demand identical across policies for a fair paired comparison, and the lost flying is costed through the AOG cost instead.

**8. Rectification windows are simplified, not MEL.** D3 and D10 are simplified analogues of MEL repair-interval categories B and C, not operator MEL decisions. They are elapsed-hour windows applied generically per part number. Real relief depends on the operator's approved MEL, the specific item, the redundancy remaining, and operational restrictions that this model does not cost.

**9. The starting-point line-station rule is a deliberate weakness of P0.** P0 puts a rotable at an outstation only where a D0 part has at least {{RESULT:config.p0_line_min}} quoted removals a year at that station, so low-volume D0 rotables (the nose wheel steering actuator, wheel-and-tyre assemblies) are not held where they fail. The D0 parts below account for {{RESULT:limits.line_station_share}} of P0's history downtime:

{{RESULT:limits.line_station_table}}

Crew oxygen masks are expendables, so the line-station rule does not apply to them: P0's min/max rule holds them at every station that sees quoted demand, and {{RESULT:limits.mask_out_share}} of the mask's history downtime was at outstations. The rule was kept on purpose (V2-SIM-16) because it is how a simple OEM-quote operation provisions, and P2 is the remedy. A more careful operator would narrow the P2 − P0 gap: {{RESULT:limits.linekit}}.

**10. Brakes: cost-per-landing contracts are not modelled.** Brakes are owned, type-specific, wear-planned rotables. Brake changes are planned at notice ({{RESULT:config.wear_notice}} ahead); residual brake downtime only occurs when AUH is short and the exchange fails. Many operators buy brakes on a cost-per-landing contract with the supplier holding the pool, which would remove brakes from the airline's capital and from this trade-off. History downtime on the brake part numbers under P0: {{RESULT:limits.brake_history}}.

**11. The shop queue is light by construction.** A unit occupies a bench slot for only {{RESULT:limits.bench_share}} of its work time (SIM-4), so shops almost never queue. The AOG Response Lab's repair queue-jump option was infeasible for lack of a queue in {{RESULT:limits.queue_jump}}. Real shops queue, prioritise AOG cores and send beyond-economic-repair quotes; none of that is modelled, so TAT variability is understated.

**12. Close to single-echelon.** Repairs happen only through the hub. Outstations are resupplied only from AUH (laterals only where a policy allows them). There is no regional warehouse, consignment stock or supplier-held pool. The analytic positioning model is METRIC-inspired, not full METRIC.

**13. Transport times are fixed planning values.** AOG transport hours per station pair are ASSUMED constants. Real next-flight-out times vary with schedule, customs, dangerous-goods rules and weight limits.

**14. Simplified scheduled maintenance.** Only A-checks at AUH generate kits. No C-checks, no modification programmes, no airworthiness directives creating sudden fleet-wide demand.

**15. Parts are independent.** No interchangeability, no alternate part numbers, no next-higher-assembly logic, no common-cause failures.

**16. Swaps and donors are simplified.** A borrowed or robbed unit is fitted at the requirement's station, and a robbed donor is assumed not to fly until it is reinstated from the next AUH unit.

## Economics and decisions

**17. Results depend heavily on the AOG recovery routes.** Every policy, P0 included, relies on robbery at AUH (which needs engineering/quality approval), local borrow at outstations, exchange/loan by fleet type (failed attempts retried {{RESULT:config.retry}}) and local buy for consumables and expendables. In practice this means access to an AOG exchange/loan market and to borrow agreements at the assumed availability and response times. Borrowed units never join the owned pool. Switching them off shows the dependence: {{RESULT:honest.routes_off}}. The frontier and the P2 − P0 gap are conditional on those markets existing.

**18. The AOG cost is a scenario.** {{RESULT:config.aog_cost}} are planning values. Real AOG cost depends on route, time of day, passenger load, crew legality and recovery options. P2's lead over P0 holds across ×0.5–×2 the hourly rate, but the frontier pick moves: {{RESULT:frontier.sensitivity.penalty_half_vs_double}}.

**19. Carrying cost is a single rate.** 20% per year (range 15–25%) covers capital cost, storage, insurance and obsolescence together.

**20. The optimiser is a greedy heuristic on an analytic proxy.** Marginal analysis gives the best allocation only for separable, convex objectives at its breakpoints. Check: {{RESULT:optim.greedy_gap_small_case_pct}}. The proxy ranks candidates but is not a forecast: {{RESULT:frontier.model_vs_sim}}. The guardrails are planner policy, not optimisation results; they cost {{RESULT:frontier.guard_cost}} of material AOG a year at P2's capital (selection seeds).

**21. The I125 frontier option is sensitive.** Its cost lead over P2 is not significant if repair is 25% faster ({{RESULT:fo.tat075_net}} a year, selection seeds). Its post-plan stress check (D015) was added after the main holdout: {{RESULT:fo.postplan_summary}}.

**22. Pool exchange (P3) is not implemented.** The AOG Response Lab shows a pool option only as "not contracted".

## Evaluation

**23. Paired seeds narrow intervals within this world only.** {{RESULT:v21.ranges}} Confidence intervals reflect simulation noise across {{RESULT:seeds.n}} seeds, not uncertainty in the assumed parameters. Parameter uncertainty is explored only through the one-at-a-time tornado, the stress worlds and the routes-off stress. Selection to holdout: {{RESULT:honest.consistency}}.

**24. The stress worlds are narrow and do not span the whole band.** They use {{RESULT:v21.stress_worlds}}. In short, {{RESULT:honest.band_ends}}. A world at 2,700 h with in-band fill was not reachable by scaling true MTBUR alone.

**25. Lower immediate fill is a real cost of the lean plans.** P2 changes immediate fill by {{RESULT:policies.P2_vs_P0.delta_fill_pts}} {{RESULT:policies.P2_vs_P0.delta_fill_pts_ci}} against P0 ({{RESULT:policies.fill_by_class}}). Consumable and expendable shortages rarely cause downtime here, but they mean more chasing on the shop floor.

**26. One fleet, one network.** Results are for a 40-aircraft, seven-station network. They are not evidence about other fleet sizes or network shapes.

## Scope

**27. Not an approved maintenance, airworthiness or dispatch tool.** It does not replace an approved MEL, an MRO/ERP system, an AOG desk, or certification paperwork (such as an authorised release certificate).

**28. Independent project.** Not affiliated with any airline, OEM, vendor or MRO organisation, and uses none of their data or systems.

## Known model limitations from the v2 domain review (disclosed, not fixed)

A fresh aviation domain review of v2 (`reports/review_aviation_v2.md`) found the model-level issues below. By the author's decision they are disclosed rather than fixed, because each fix would need another rebuild, recalibration and holdout. The wording and UI issues from the same review were fixed.

**29. Robbed-donor downtime is not counted (AV2-01).** A robbed donor aircraft is assumed not to fly until it is reinstated, but those hours are not counted as material AOG. In the P0 history there are about {{RESULT:limits.robbery_per_yr}} robberies a year, donors are held for {{RESULT:limits.donor_days_mean}} on average, and the donor hours come to about {{RESULT:limits.donor_h_per_yr}} a year, against {{RESULT:limits.p0_history_aog_per_yr}} counted material AOG hours. {{RESULT:limits.robbery_d3d10_share}} of robberies are for D3/D10 items. Absolute downtime is therefore understated for every policy. P0 robs more often than P2, so the P2-vs-P0 gap is probably understated too, but the absolute levels should not be quoted as fleet downtime.

**30. P0's outstation stock is mostly unusable (AV2-02).** {{RESULT:limits.p0_out_brakes}} of P0's {{RESULT:limits.p0_out_total}} outstation rotables are wear-planned brakes, and every brake change is done at AUH, so only the rest can ever be used at a line station. P0 holds no nose-wheel or widebody wheel assemblies at outstations and never re-provisions over the two years. Most of P0's downtime comes from no-go actuators and wheels at line stations, so the P2-vs-P0 headline largely measures "stock no-go items at line stations". A P0 with a standard line kit was measured during calibration but not adopted (only the fewest-changes variant met the band); it would narrow the gap.

**31. Wheels and tyres are modelled as rare unscheduled removals (AV2-04).** In reality they are the highest-volume rotable, and most changes are wear-planned.

**32. Some brake positioning comes from a station-share prior (AV2-05).** P2 and I125 place a few brakes at outstations where no brake change ever happens, which is dead stock. {{RESULT:v21.brakes_invalid}}

**33. The hero part's removal rate is high, and there is no warranty or reliability escalation (AV2-06).** A real planner would escalate a part removed this often to reliability and warranty before buying more spares. The product now says so: "{{RESULT:v21.hero_part}}"

**34. Robbery is the engine's automatic first route at AUH, at a flat availability (AV2-07).** In practice robbery is a case-by-case engineering decision and would often follow an exchange or loan. The AOG Response Lab never ranks robbery, but the simulated policies use it.

**35. Deferral has no cost of its own (AV2-08).** Flying with a D3/D10 item deferred costs nothing until the window expires, so the optimiser can cut expensive deferrable floats more readily than an operator would.

**36. AOG hours run from removal, not from the next scheduled departure (AV2-09).** {{RESULT:v21.aog_clock}}

**37. Exchange and loan are one route, and there is no pool or component-support contract (AV2-12).** A widebody operator would usually have a pool or support agreement that changes the widebody sourcing picture.

**38. Unit costs are drawn per part family, not per aircraft type (AV2-13).** Several are well off typical list prices, so absolute capital figures, including I125's extra investment, are illustrative. Comparisons within the model are consistent, but the AED amounts are not price estimates.

**39. Other simplifications noted by the AI-assisted domain review ([reports/review_aviation_v2.md](reports/review_aviation_v2.md)).** Some removal rates are high, local buy of windshields is very fast, IDG/VFSG repair is in-house, outstations are assumed to have fitting capability, robbery at AUH is not used to supply outstations, and P1 is a deliberately simple comparator.

**40. Outstation wheels (A9).** {{RESULT:v21.wheels_note}}

**41. Donor downtime is kept out of the headline.** {{RESULT:v21.donor_body}}

